Import safety rules
- Never clear or overwrite existing user data automatically.
- Import should be idempotent: retrying the same backup must not duplicate records. Use migrationId + source collection + source document ID or an equivalent deterministic key.
- Provide a dry-run preview before writes.
- Validate every record; do not trust JSON content just because it came from the old app.
- Never import Firebase authentication tokens, credentials, or secrets.
- Do not blindly reuse old public share tokens. Recreate share links only after an explicit user action, using secure token generation and the new security model.
- Keep original dates, numeric amounts, historical fee values, transaction direction, and document relationships. Do not recalculate historical transfers with today's fee settings.
- Keep a local copy of the selected file under the user's control; the app should not claim it has preserved the file unless it actually has.
- If import fails midway, resume safely or offer a controlled rollback of only the records created by that migration. Never delete unrelated records.
- If the old version has no export feature or the file is not a valid backup, explain the problem and show how to create the required backup in the old version. Do not pretend automatic migration is possible.
2.4 Exact legacy backup contract: eco-backup version 1
This section is authoritative for importing backups created by the old version's Settings → Store Backup button. It replaces any earlier illustrative backup schema in this blueprint. Do not assume the backup uses a simplified sourceApp, complete, or plain-object schema: the real contract uses app, integrity, and {id, fields} records.
File-level contract
- UTF-8 JSON, pretty-printed with two-space indentation, MIME type application/json.
- Filename: eco-backup-YYYY-MM-DD-HH-mm-ss.json; the filename timestamp is UTC and represents the same instant as exportedAt.
- exportedAt is an ISO-8601 UTC string ending in Z.
- The export is for the currently signed-in user's own users/{uid}/… data and deliberately contains no source UID.
- The old exporter reads from Firestore server, not the offline cache. If any read or serialization fails, it produces no file; a present file has integrity.complete: true.
- Export is read-only and must not write to or modify Firestore.
Required top-level shape
{
  "format": "eco-backup",
  "formatVersion": 1,
  "exportedAt": "2026-10-21T08:49:20.000Z",
  "app": {
    "name": "EcO (Echo)",
    "purpose": "Personal finance tracker – full per-user data backup"
  },
  "timestampEncoding": "Human-readable note describing tagged Firestore values",
  "data": {
    "settings": [],
    "expenses": [],
    "recurringExpenses": [],
    "earnings": [],
    "transfers": [],
    "openingBalances": [],
    "sources": [],
    "people": [],
    "transactions": [],
    "shareLinks": [],
    "pendingRequests": []
  },
  "integrity": {
    "complete": true,
    "counts": {
      "settings": 1,
      "expenses": 0,
      "recurringExpenses": 0,
      "earnings": 0,
      "transfers": 0,
      "openingBalances": 1,
      "sources": 0,
      "people": 0,
      "transactions": 0,
      "shareLinks": 0,
      "pendingRequests": 0
    },
    "redactedFields": {},
    "excluded": [
      "shareHashes",
      "issueReports",
      "userDirectory",
      "connectionInvites",
      "connections",
      "sharedTransactions",
      "voidRequests",
      "admins",
      "adminAuditLog"
    ]
  }
}
The example values are illustrative; real counts and redaction entries depend on the actual export. data.<collection> arrays contain records shaped exactly as follows:
{
  "id": "original-firestore-document-id",
  "fields": {
    "amount": 123.45,
    "date": "2026-10-21"
  }
}
id is the original Firestore document ID. fields contains the original document fields without renaming, recalculation, rounding, or normalization. Preserve unknown fields for forward compatibility. Array ordering has no semantic meaning.
Exported collections and legacy meanings
All listed collections are exported from users/{uid}/<collection>.
Collection	Contract and compatibility notes
settings	Normally one record with ID main. Known fields include theme, setupComplete, budgetMode, directDailyBudget, monthlyIncome, monthlyFixed, monthlySavings, monthlyEmergency, bufferPct, cashoutChargeRate, categories, hiddenDefaultCategories, tags, and any other fields present.
expenses	Known fields include reason, category, description, amount, type (regular/fixed/emergency), payMethod, date, timestamp.
recurringExpenses	Expense-like fields plus perDay, days, startDate, endDate, monthly.
earnings	Known fields include source, description, amount, payMethod, date, timestamp.
transfers	Known fields include from, to, requestedAmount, chargeApplied, chargeAmount, sourceDeducted, chargeRate, note, date, timestamp. Preserve historical fee values.
openingBalances	Known fields include cash, bkash, nagad, note, date, timestamp. Normally one record with ID current.
sources	Known fields include name, description, timestamp.
people	Known fields include name and createdAt; createdAt is used instead of timestamp.
transactions	Known fields include person, personId, type (give/take), amount, totalAmount, chargeApplied, chargeAmount, chargeType, payMethod, tag, description, date, isOpening, fromRequest, timestamp.
shareLinks	Known fields include personId, person, createdAt, and hash; the hash is redacted as specified below.
pendingRequests	Known fields include person, personId, type, amount, payMethod, tag, submitterNote, date, status, submittedAt, resolvedAt. submitterNote is free text and must be rendered safely.


These are known fields, not allowlists. Exporters copy all existing fields, including newer fields such as isLinked, fromUID, toUID, linkedTxnId, voided, and voidRequestId if present. Importers must tolerate missing and unknown fields.
Special-value encoding
Normal JSON strings, booleans, finite numbers, null, arrays, and nested objects stay normal JSON. Firestore special types are encoded as tagged objects identified by __type:
Original value	JSON representation
Firestore Timestamp	{"__type":"timestamp","value":"2023-11-14T22:13:20.000Z","seconds":1700000000,"nanoseconds":0}
JavaScript Date	{"__type":"timestamp","value":"<ISO UTC>"}
GeoPoint	{"__type":"geopoint","latitude":...,"longitude":...}
DocumentReference	{"__type":"reference","path":"users/…/…"}
Bytes	{"__type":"bytes","base64":"…"}
NaN / positive or negative Infinity	{"__type":"number","value":"NaN"} or the corresponding string form


Restore tagged values recursively, including inside arrays and nested objects. Rebuild Firestore timestamps from seconds and nanoseconds for exactness; use value only if seconds are absent. Date-only strings such as "2026-10-21" remain plain strings. If an old export encounters an unrepresentable value such as undefined or an unknown type, export fails instead of guessing.
Share-link redaction and reconstruction
shareLinks.hash is a reusable public access token, so the exporter sets every exported fields.hash to null. If a share-link document ID was itself equal to its hash, the exported id is replaced by redacted-<n>, where n is its position in the array. integrity.redactedFields contains {"shareLinks.hash": <count>} when values were removed.

EcO 2.0 must not attempt to recover old hashes. After import, offer to create fresh secure share links for the same personId/person and tell the user that old links are no longer valid/recoverable from the backup. Do not activate or publish new links without explicit user confirmation.
Intentionally excluded from the backup
- Top-level shareHashes and issueReports.
- userDirectory, connectionInvites, connections, sharedTransactions, and voidRequests, because these involve other users or shared-account data.
- admins and adminAuditLog.
- Firebase credentials, API keys, auth tokens, passwords, and other secrets.
- The counterpart copy of a linked transaction stored in another user's account.
Do not tell users that importing this backup restores mutual connections or the other person's account-side copy of linked transactions. Those are intentionally absent; users must reconnect and establish new shared events in EcO 2.0.
Required importer validation
1. Parse JSON and require format === "eco-backup".
2. Support formatVersion === 1; reject unsupported versions with a clear message. Do not silently attempt a best-effort import of a version the importer does not understand.
3. Require integrity.complete === true.
4. For every expected collection, require a valid array and verify data[name].length === integrity.counts[name]. Report malformed/missing count entries as validation errors rather than treating them as zero.
5. Validate each record has a string id and object fields; reject duplicate IDs within a collection or explain a deterministic collision strategy before writes.
6. Restore tagged special values recursively and validate the tag's shape before constructing native values.
7. Map old field names to the EcO 2.0 schema in the importer/adapter, never by modifying the selected backup file.
8. Preserve original IDs where safe. Where the new schema requires different IDs, store a deterministic source-ID mapping and rewrite only the references explicitly covered by that mapping.
9. Import people before features that need to resolve personId, even if the physical write order is otherwise independent.
10. Make retries idempotent with a stable migration ID and deterministic source-record mapping. A retry must not create duplicate financial events.
11. Use bounded batches and respect Firestore write limits. Record progress so interrupted imports can resume safely.
12. Re-read imported records and verify counts and key totals before reporting success.
13. Recreate share links with new hashes only after consent.
14. The old exporter was tested only against a mocked Firestore in Node—not verified against a live database or browser. Before launch, test with a genuine backup from the old app, including timestamps, redaction, all collections, failure-aborts-with-no-file, and a full restore/verification round trip.
The selected backup file must be treated as untrusted input even if it claims to be an EcO export. Do not import executable content or render user-supplied strings as HTML. A successful parse is not proof of a valid or safe backup.
