12. Shared JavaScript Architecture
Preserve the existing vanilla HTML/CSS/JS structure unless a specific requirement justifies a change. Keep Firebase Auth and Firestore as the core services.
Recommended shared modules
The existing _shared.js can be gradually organized into clear responsibilities without requiring a framework:
- Firebase initialization and authenticated-user state.
- Common navigation/theme shell.
- Currency/date formatting.
- Firestore data access helpers.
- Payment-method registry and legacy name mapping.
- Expense/earning/transfer/transaction validation.
- Financial calculation functions.
- Global Add modal state machine and workflow definitions.
- Unified List normalization/filter/sort/pagination.
- Toast, confirmation, accessible modal, and error handling.
- Backup export/import validation and migration orchestration.
- Notification inbox UI.
- Audit/event helpers.
Avoid implementing four independent versions of the same fee, balance, validation, or payment-method logic. Business logic should be testable as pure functions wherever possible.
Data loading and performance
- Avoid loading every expense for every dashboard render.
- Use bounded queries and pagination for large histories.
- Cache settings and method configuration within the current authenticated session, then invalidate affected caches after writes.
- Use real-time listeners only where they materially improve proposal/notification status; detach listeners when no longer needed.
- Use batched writes for imports where limits permit; chunk large imports and track progress.
- Treat offline state explicitly. Do not report a write as saved until Firestore confirms it.
- Use stable IDs and deterministic sorting.
13. Proposed Firestore Data Model
This section proposes extensions, not a claim that these paths already exist. Preserve existing collections and field meanings during migration wherever possible.
Existing user-owned data (preserve)
- users/{uid}/settings/main
- users/{uid}/expenses/{expenseId}
- users/{uid}/recurringExpenses/{recurringId}
- users/{uid}/earnings/{earningId}
- users/{uid}/transfers/{transferId}
