Do not store the entire backup redundantly in the migration job.
Stable source mapping
users/{uid}/migrationJobs/{migrationId}/mappings/{mappingId}

Store source collection, source document ID, target collection/document ID, and outcome so retries are idempotent.
User directory / public code
Use a minimal controlled directory document keyed by a normalized unique code, mapping to a UID and safe display information. Never allow clients to write arbitrary UID mappings. Do not expose email or private account data through code lookup.
Connections
A canonical connection document should identify both participants, status, creation/activation timestamps, and the participant-local person IDs. Both users' access should be restricted to the connection and explicitly shared events.
Transaction proposals
A proposal record should include:
- connectionId
- senderUid
- recipientUid
- senderPersonId
- recipientPersonId
- status
- amount, date, direction, paymentMethodId or approved snapshot, tag, note
- createdAt, updatedAt, expiresAt
- decisionAt, decisionBy
- finalizedLocalAt when applicable
- idempotencyKey
- canonical sharedEventId after acceptance
- audit metadata
The exact path can be connection-scoped or top-level, but rules must prove that only the participants can read the record and only the recipient can accept/reject it.
Canonical shared event
Create one canonical event for an accepted shared transaction, with a unique event ID and a reference to the originating proposal. Each user's UI renders the event from their own perspective. Do not create two independent confirmed events that both represent the same event in the same ledger calculation.
Notifications
users/{uid}/notifications/{notificationId}

Store type, related entity ID, created/read timestamps, and a minimal safe message payload. Notifications are UI records; they do not themselves authorize financial changes.
Audit log
Use an append-only audit trail for connection activation, proposal decisions, local-only finalization, corrections, and voids. Restrict writes to trusted operations where the action affects both users.
Data-model rule
Avoid a full rewrite of historical records solely to make the new UI easier. Use adapters/mappings at read time where safe, and run explicit, versioned migrations only when necessary.
14. Financial Rules and Calculation Integrity
Financial logic is the highest-risk area. Before coding, extract formulas from the current source and establish golden test cases. Do not use the following conceptual rules as a substitute for that source audit.
Core distinctions
- Expense: reduces the relevant wallet balance and contributes to spending statistics.
- Earning: increases the selected wallet and contributes to earnings.
- Transfer: moves money between wallets; it is neither new income nor spending. A qualifying fee may be counted according to the established rules.
- Peer transaction: affects the relevant wallet movement and receivable/payable ledger according to the existing give/take semantics.
- Pending shared proposal: has no effect on confirmed balances.
- Accepted shared event: is represented once and interpreted from each participant's perspective.
- Local-only fallback: affects only the sender's local ledger after explicit choice or the 48-hour timeout.
Wallet total versus net/actual balance
Keep these separate in labels and explanations. The existing project context records a historical test case:
- Opening balances: Cash ৳1,040; bKash ৳8,000; Nagad ৳0.
- Before lending: wallet total and net total are ৳9,040.
- User lends ৳500 via bKash.
- Expected wallet total remains ৳9,040 under the previously intended receivable interpretation; net/actual total becomes ৳8,540 under that specific historical expectation.
This case must be reconciled with the exact current implementation and ledger convention before release. The blueprint must not “fix” it by guessing. Add custom wallets to the same verified calculation engine.
Calculation safeguards
- Use numeric values internally; format currency only for display.
- Define rounding at the calculation boundary.
- Preserve historical fee values on transfers.
- Handle 0% fee as a valid value.
- Define whether dates are local calendar dates and avoid UTC conversion off-by-one errors in Bangladesh time.
- Ensure peer principal and fees are not double-counted.
- Pending proposals never appear in confirmed statistics.
- Regression-test old and new records together.
15. Notifications, Reminders, and Background Processing
Every signed-in user has a notification bar/inbox, accessible from the application shell on desktop and mobile. The bell should show an unread indicator/count, open a chronological list, and link each item to the relevant activity or action.
15.1 Notification inbox rules
- Show only notifications from the last seven days.
- Notifications older than seven days are automatically deleted; they are not merely hidden forever in the UI.
- Define retention using a trusted creation timestamp (createdAt) and a consistent server-side cutoff. A scheduled cleanup job should delete expired notification documents in bounded batches.
- The client may filter the visible list to the seven-day window for immediate UX, but client filtering alone does not satisfy automatic deletion.
- Mark-as-read changes notification metadata only; it must not accept, reject, create, or edit a financial transaction.
- Sort newest first. Provide useful empty, loading, offline, and failure states.
- Notifications are informational pointers, not proof that a financial event was accepted or completed. Always re-read and validate the linked proposal/event before enabling actions.
- Keep notification payloads minimal. Do not put sensitive ledger details or another user's full transaction history into a push payload.
- If an event is older than seven days, its notification is removed, but the underlying financial record or proposal follows its own retention/status policy. Notification cleanup must never delete a transaction.
Suggested document shape:
{
  "type": "transaction_proposal_received",
  "title": "A transaction needs your review",
  "body": "Review a proposed shared transaction in EcO.",
  "createdAt": "Firestore Timestamp",
  "readAt": null,
  "relatedType": "transactionProposal",
  "relatedId": "proposal-id",
  "expiresAt": "Firestore Timestamp"
}
This is a proposed notification schema, not a claim about existing stored fields. The seven-day expiration can be calculated by a trusted backend as createdAt + 7 days. Use Firestore TTL only if the deployed database supports and has TTL configured for the chosen expiration field; otherwise use a scheduled cleanup function. Do not rely on browser timers, because the user may close the app or remain offline.
15.2 Notification types
Potential in-app notifications include:
- Connection request received, accepted, or declined.
- Shared transaction proposal received.
- Shared transaction accepted, rejected, cancelled, or corrected.
- A reminder that a transaction proposal is waiting for review.
- A transaction finalized as sender-only/local after the 48-hour response window.
- A correction/void request update.
- Import completed or failed.
- Optional security notices such as a newly created share link.
Do not create repetitive notifications for every refresh or every read. Use deterministic event IDs/idempotency keys so retries cannot flood a user's inbox.
15.3 Scheduling and trusted execution
Scheduled reminders, automatic 48-hour finalization, and seven-day deletion require a trusted scheduler/backend or a correctly configured managed TTL mechanism. A browser-only timer is not reliable enough. Every scheduled task must re-check the current state, be idempotent, and do nothing if the proposal was already resolved, cancelled, or otherwise changed. Cross-account financial state changes must use trusted atomic operations.
15.4 WhatsApp money reminder for people who are not mutually connected
When a user has lent money to a person who is not mutually connected on EcO, the lender may optionally create a WhatsApp reminder. This is a user-initiated communication tool, not an automatic message sender.
User flow
1. From the relevant person/loan detail or transaction row, the lender chooses Remind via WhatsApp.
2. EcO asks for or uses the person's WhatsApp phone number. Validate country code/number format and show the full number for confirmation before opening WhatsApp. Do not require a number to record the loan.
3. Generate a personalized, formatted message from the verified transaction/person context. The wording should be polite, factual, and non-threatening, for example:
   Hi [person name], just a friendly reminder from [lender name] about the ৳[amount] I lent you on [date]. When convenient, could you please let me know when I can expect repayment? Thank you.
4. Show a preview with an editable message field. The lender can change the wording, amount reference, or greeting before sending.
5. The explicit action button should say Copy message & open WhatsApp or similarly unambiguous wording.
6. On click, copy the message to the clipboard and open a WhatsApp chat deep link for the confirmed phone number. Use an encoded https://wa.me/<countrycodephonenumber>?text=<URL-encoded-message> URL when supported by the browser/platform.
7. WhatsApp may prefill the message in the chat composer, depending on device/browser behavior. The user must review and press WhatsApp's own Send button. EcO must not claim it can automatically paste/send in all environments, and it must never send a message without the user's deliberate action.
8. If clipboard access fails or is blocked, show the message in a selectable text area with a Copy message fallback and still offer to open WhatsApp.
9. After returning to EcO, the user may optionally record “Reminder opened/sent by me” as a private local status. Do not mark the debt as repaid or the reminder as delivered merely because WhatsApp opened.
Message and privacy rules
- Do not send WhatsApp messages from the EcO backend or pretend the message was sent by an official EcO account. The message may be branded in a transparent way such as “A reminder from [lender name] using EcO”; it must not impersonate EcO support or a system notification.
- The lender chooses whether to open WhatsApp and whether to send.
- Do not expose the lender's other financial records, the recipient's account data, or a public share token in the message.
- Allow the user to edit the text and verify the recipient number before opening WhatsApp.
- Avoid repeated or harassing reminders. Provide a simple last-reminder timestamp/cooldown and let the lender decide when another reminder is appropriate.
- Phone numbers are personal data. Store them only if necessary, explain why, protect them with access rules, and provide edit/delete controls. Prefer per-person optional contact details rather than making them mandatory for all people.
- If the person is mutually connected on EcO, guide the lender toward the in-app notification/proposal workflow instead. Do not silently send both a WhatsApp message and an EcO notification for the same action.
15.5 Connected-user notifications
If both people have EcO accounts and have established a mutual connection, use an in-app notification for the intended recipient when a shared transaction proposal or other supported event requires their attention.
- Notify the recipient only after the trusted backend confirms the proposal was created for the correct active connection.
- A notification does not mean the recipient has accepted the proposal. Pending proposals do not change the recipient's confirmed balance.
- The recipient can open the notification, inspect details, and explicitly accept or reject the proposal.
- For a confirmed, accepted shared event, notify each participant only where useful and avoid duplicate alerts.
- Browser/system push is optional and permission-based. The in-app inbox remains the source of truth.
Delivery levels
- In-app notification record: required.
- Bell/inbox and unread count: required.
- Seven-day automatic cleanup: required; use managed TTL or scheduled backend cleanup.
- Real-time refresh: useful for active sessions; use listeners only where valuable and detach them correctly.
- Scheduled reminders/deadlines: require trusted scheduled execution.
- Browser push: optional enhancement; never the only notification mechanism.
16. Security, Privacy, and Reliability
- Require authentication for private financial data.
- Enforce user ownership in Firestore rules, not just UI logic.
- Prevent clients from assigning themselves another user's UID or connection identity.
- Validate connection code lookup through controlled backend/rules design.
- Only participants can read a connection/proposal/shared event.
- Only the intended recipient can accept/reject a proposal.
- Use trusted atomic operations for connection activation, acceptance, duplicate prevention, timeout finalization, and cross-user changes.
- Never allow a pending proposal to alter the recipient's balance.
- Keep unrelated expenses, earnings, wallets, settings, and full account history private.
- Use safe DOM text rendering and avoid raw HTML injection from names, descriptions, tags, and notes.
- Use high-entropy share tokens; provide revocation and least-privilege rules.
- Rate-limit or otherwise protect public request endpoints where applicable.
- Confirm destructive operations and keep audit history for shared events.
- Provide clear error states for permission denied, offline, malformed imports, and partial failures.
The current app is documented as mostly client-side. The proposed cross-account approval and scheduled timeout features require limited trusted backend functionality. This must be stated honestly in the architecture and deployment plan.
17. Data Export, Import, and Migration Strategy
Export
Provide Settings → Data & Migration → Export Backup. Export all supported user-owned data and settings, including original IDs and timestamps, with:
- format identifier/version;
- export timestamp;
- record counts;
- completeness flag;
- consistent timestamp serialization;
- no auth tokens or secrets;
- explicit notes about data that cannot be exported (for example, internal server-only audit details).
The export must not depend on which List filters are active.
Import
Use the first-launch existing-user flow, and also allow future imports from Settings. Import must:
1. Parse locally.
2. Validate format/version and schema.
3. Show preview/warnings.
4. Require authentication.
5. Require confirmation.
6. Run idempotently in chunks.
7. Record migration job status.
8. Verify counts and core financial totals.
9. Present a report.
10. Never overwrite unrelated data silently.
Exact legacy field and semantic mapping requirements
The eco-backup v1 contract exports data.settings as an array of {id, fields} records (normally one record with ID main) and data.openingBalances as an array (normally one record with ID current). Do not treat either as a plain object or null.
- Preserve the original record ID and every original field until validation is complete.
- transactions.type values are give and take; records with isOpening: true have inverted meaning in the legacy UI logic. Test this explicitly rather than applying the normal transaction rule to opening entries.
- Preserve amount separately from totalAmount. Preserve chargeApplied, chargeAmount, and chargeType as distinct historical facts.
- Resolve transaction-person relationships by personId first, then fall back to the person name only when the ID cannot be resolved. The people document IDs are the targets of personId.
- Preserve fromRequest and linked-transaction fields where present. A backup may include fields newer than the list of known fields.
- Tagged Firestore values can occur anywhere inside nested objects/arrays. Do not restore only top-level timestamps.
- Do not expect old public share tokens: shareLinks.fields.hash is null, and an ID may be a redacted-<n> placeholder.
- The backup excludes cross-account collections and the other user's counterpart transaction. Importing a file cannot by itself restore old mutual connections or reconstruct a missing counterpart copy.
- Keep the backup file unchanged. Perform every compatibility transformation in a versioned import adapter and retain a migration report describing warnings and mappings.
Mapping old to new
- Map Cash, bKash, and Nagad to stable built-in payment method IDs.
- Preserve unknown payment method names as unresolved legacy values for user review; do not map them to a random built-in method.
- Preserve old document IDs when no collision exists; otherwise map old IDs to new IDs and retain source references.
- Preserve person references and update references only through an explicit mapping table.
- Preserve old fee amounts/rates, dates, and historical transaction direction.
- Import settings carefully: new defaults fill missing fields only; they must not replace present old settings.
- Recreate share links only after user consent; do not automatically activate legacy public tokens.
- Connected-user data from the old version should not be inferred from local person names.
Verification report
Show expected/imported/skipped/failed counts, duplicate handling, unresolved records, and checksums/totals where useful. Do not claim success solely because writes returned without error; perform a post-import read/verification.
