Phase 1 — Backup contract and migration
- Implement/export the versioned JSON backup in the old version if not already available.
- Implement new-version file validation, preview, import job, ID mapping, idempotency, and verification.
- Test malformed, partial, duplicate, large, and retry scenarios.
Gate: import preserves data and never silently duplicates or overwrites.
Phase 2 — Shared payment-method registry
- Add stable built-in IDs and custom method documents.
- Migrate legacy strings safely.
- Update all selectors and wallet calculations to use one registry.
- Add archive/rename rules and opening-balance handling.
Gate: all old balance tests pass with built-in and custom methods.
Phase 3 — Global Add modal
- Implement shared modal shell and state machine.
- Move four workflows into it, reusing existing business logic.
- Add inline creation for people, tags, and sources.
- Add keyboard/accessibility/duplicate-submit behavior.
Gate: each workflow works from every authenticated page.
Phase 4 — Unified List
- Normalize records from all existing collections into a display model.
- Add filters, search, pagination, type-specific rows, and actions.
- Retire redundant activity lists only after parity testing.
Gate: all record types appear once, with correct filtering and edit/delete behavior.
Phase 5 — Dashboard, Wallet, Statistics, Settings refinement
- Connect dashboard summaries to verified calculation functions.
- Add custom wallet cards and method-aware statistics.
- Add data migration and backup controls to Settings.
- Preserve existing six stats sections unless explicitly redesigned.
Gate: no discrepancy between List, Wallet, Dashboard, and Statistics.
Phase 6 — Connections and proposals
- Design and test connection code, mutual request, authorization rules, proposal states, canonical shared events, corrections, and audit logs.
- Add limited trusted backend operations for acceptance and scheduled timeouts.
- Test concurrency, duplicate acceptance, recipient privacy, and late response behavior.
Gate: shared events are authorized, auditable, and counted exactly once.
Phase 7 — Security, performance, and release
- Review Firestore rules and public share access.
- Test XSS-safe rendering, large datasets, offline/retry paths, accessibility, and mobile layout.
- Verify service worker/manifest and cache update strategy.
- Deploy to a test Firebase project before production.
- Provide rollback and backup instructions.
