23.11 Currency, dates, and locale
- Currency: Bangladeshi Taka, ৳.
- Two decimal places for stored monetary values and financial calculations.
- Use Indian/Bangladeshi digit grouping where specified by the product, e.g. ৳ 1,62,450.00, not ৳ 162,450.00. Use a tested formatter; do not manually concatenate separators.
- Timezone: Asia/Dhaka (UTC+6).
- Keep date-only values as date-only strings where the data model requires them; do not shift a date by converting it carelessly through UTC.
- Use Inter for labels and JetBrains Mono for money/timestamps/IDs.
- Use concise date labels such as “Today” in normal views; full timestamps belong in details.
23.12 Payment-method domain model and duplicate naming
- New account starts with a single Cash method.
- Every additional method is user-created.
- Do not require a phone/account number.
- Suggested record shape (adapt to the actual architecture without breaking legacy import):
  - stable id
  - name
  - optional type metadata
  - isDefault or equivalent
  - archived status
  - creation/update metadata where appropriate
- Optional type metadata must not dictate brand colors.
- Duplicate names are allowed as distinct records. Generate display names Name, Name~2, Name~3, etc.
- Duplicate matching should ignore case and surrounding whitespace when deciding whether a name is a duplicate.
- Preserve stable IDs and references. Renaming a method must not silently rewrite historical transactions or change which record they reference.
- Avoid physical deletion when historical records depend on a method; offer archive/disable or a safe migration.
- On import, inspect the legacy opening balance fields and transaction payMethod values. Create/restore only the payment methods represented in the imported backup. If bkash is present but nagad is absent, do not add Nagad. If both are present, restore both. If the backup has only Cash, keep only Cash. Do not infer method existence merely from a generic capability list.
- Historical references such as legacy names must be mapped deterministically to the correct method IDs. Show any ambiguous mapping in the import preview and require resolution rather than guessing.
- Do not show masked example phone numbers in sample UI.
23.13 Interaction patterns
- Interactive element states: default, hover/focus, pressed, disabled, loading, success/error as relevant.
- Touch feedback must not rely on fragile global event handlers alone; use CSS :active and proper button semantics.
- Dropdowns close on outside click and Escape; controls are keyboard accessible.
- Progress animations should be subtle and respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Live sync indicators must reflect real connection/sync state; do not show “Online • Synced” as decorative fiction.
- Search, filters, sort, pagination, export, and detail toggles must actually work.
- Prefer event listeners and modular functions over inline onclick attributes.
- Use aria-label, labels for inputs, logical focus order, and visible focus styles.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion.
