This is a placeholder, not working credentials. Keep initialization isolated. If values remain placeholders, show a helpful setup error rather than endlessly loading or falsely showing an authenticated state.

The owner will add the actual Firebase details manually.
24.2 Firebase rules, indexes, and security are owner-managed
Do not attempt to apply Firebase Rules or create indexes through the generated browser application. Instead produce FIREBASE_SETUP.md with:
- Every collection/document path the app reads or writes.
- Every query shape that may require a composite index.
- A proposed least-privilege rules design and a clear note that the owner must review/paste/deploy it manually.
- Authenticated-user ownership requirements for users/{uid}/....
- Explicitly defined access for connection invitations, mutually approved connections, proposals, shared records, public share views, and void/correction requests.
- No broad public reads of private transactions, share-link records, or documents exposing owner UIDs.
- Public share access only through high-entropy, unguessable tokens and a minimal read-only projection; never expose an entire private collection by accident.
- Rules emulator test scenarios for allowed and denied reads/writes.
- Manual steps for index deployment and handling missing-index errors.
- A clear statement that client-side checks are not security enforcement.
Do not make rules “open” to bypass permission errors. If the current design requires privileged atomic cross-account operations, document the required trusted backend function/API and do not simulate it with unsafe direct writes.
24.3 Trusted backend boundary
Some features need a trusted backend or managed scheduled service:
- atomic acceptance/finalization of cross-account transaction proposals;
- secure creation/validation of share tokens if it requires privileged checks;
- scheduled notification deletion after seven days;
- 48-hour proposal expiry/finalization;
- any action that must run while no user's browser is open.
Design client interfaces and documented callable contracts for these features. Do not claim they work end-to-end until the owner configures the required backend. If backend code is requested later, keep it separate from client code and never expose credentials.
25. Page-by-Page Implementation Requirements
The product blueprint earlier in this file defines the user journeys, fields, calculations, and data compatibility. These page requirements explain how to implement the entire site while preserving the minimal Institutional Emerald UI.
25.1 Auth entry and Dashboard (index.html)
- Handle signed-out, loading, authenticated, and auth-error states.
- The signed-out state offers Firebase sign-in options supported by the project owner's configuration.
- After authentication, check whether first-launch onboarding/migration is complete.
- Dashboard normal view: Net Total, Wallet Total, remaining daily budget, compact wallet balances, recent activity, and concise receivable/payable summary.
- Avoid showing all metrics at once. Each major number can open a relevant detailed view.
- Include one prominent global Add (+) action.
- Do not invent “growth vs last month” or other insights unless the calculation and data support it.
- Do not display fake sync status.
25.2 Onboarding and migration (onboarding.html)
- New user vs previous EcO user.
- Local JSON selection, parse/validate, preview, explicit confirmation, idempotent import, progress, verification report.
- Do not overwrite data silently.
- Cash-only default for a truly new user.
- Existing user import restores only methods actually represented by the backup and preserves historical references.
- A backup with legacy opening-balance fields cash, bkash, nagad needs careful mapping to the new payment-method model. Do not lose historical balances or create absent methods.
- Settings from import take precedence over setup defaults; fill only genuinely missing preferences.
25.3 Unified Add (+) workflow
One entry point, adaptive steps:
- Expense
- Income/Earning
- Transfer
- Give
- Take
Only show fields relevant to the selected type. Use progressive disclosure for notes and advanced options.

Expense: amount, category, payment method, date, optional note/description.

Income: amount, source, destination payment method, date, optional note.

Transfer: source method, destination method, amount, applicable fee/rate and net amount received before confirmation. Preserve old fee fields for historical transfers; never recompute history from current rates.

Give/Take: person, amount, direction, payment method where relevant, date, optional note/tag, and only the fields required by the established ledger model.
- Validate positive finite amounts and required fields.
- Prevent transfer from and to the same method unless the original semantics explicitly allow it.
- Explain a fee before committing.
- Keep Save/Cancel obvious.
- Prevent accidental double submission.
- Show success only after Firestore confirms the write.
- After save, refresh only the relevant data or update a well-defined cache.
- Support keyboard and mobile one-handed use.
25.4 Unified List (list.html)
- One searchable/filterable activity history across supported activity types.
- Filter by date range, transaction type, payment method, category (where applicable), and person (where applicable).
- Search and filters must operate on actual data, not just visual chips.
- Compact rows: title/category, amount, concise date/context. Full metadata is in details.
- Group by day when it improves scanning.
- Use bounded queries/pagination; do not load every expense forever.
- Empty state, no-results state, loading, offline, and permission-denied states.
- Export controls must do real work or be clearly disabled with an explanation.
25.5 Statistics (stats.html)
Maintain the established six-section stats concept unless source verification shows otherwise:
1. Overview.
2. Category analysis.
3. Trends.
4. Budget analysis.
5. Income/expense.
6. Insights.
Normal view shows a concise period summary and one useful visualization/summary at a time. Deeper breakdowns are on demand. Do not fabricate comparisons, percentages, or data points. Correctly handle no data, zero denominators, date boundaries, recurring expenses, and category changes.
25.6 Wallets / payment methods (wallets.html)
- Cash exists by default; other methods are user-created.
- One unified payment-method color/icon system.
- Normal list shows method name and current balance only, with a subtle details affordance.
- Details contain opening balance, earnings, expenses, transfers, applicable fees, activity history, and safe management controls.
- Add method, rename, archive/disable, and set default where appropriate.
- Use stable IDs and duplicate display naming (Name, Name~2, Name~3).
- No phone/account number field is required.
- Keep wallet total distinct from Net Total.
- Transfers between the user's own methods are neither income nor expense.
- Preserve legacy historical names and references through an explicit mapping layer.
25.7 People and personal ledger (people.html / transaction.html or equivalent)
- Normal list shows person name, current balance, and unambiguous direction.
- Person detail shows chronological Give/Take history, balance calculation, relevant notes, and available actions.
- Support people who do not have EcO accounts.
- The app must not state that the user owes a person when the ledger says the person owes the user.
- WhatsApp reminders are optional, user-initiated, editable, and never auto-sent. Show the message preview and confirmed number before opening WhatsApp. Opening WhatsApp does not prove the message was sent or the debt was repaid.
- Any sharing of a personal ledger requires explicit scope and permission.
25.8 Connections and shared transaction proposals
- Support request/accept/reject flow for EcO-to-EcO connections.
- Shared transactions are proposed first and become mutually recorded only after required approval.
- Show the proposal's person, amount, direction, and relevant context before the recipient decides.
- Pending proposals must be visibly distinct from accepted/finalized records.
- Prevent duplicates using deterministic IDs/idempotency.
- Revalidate proposal state before actions.
- Cross-account updates must use trusted atomic backend operations or an explicitly safe, documented equivalent.
- Do not expose the user's whole ledger when a single transaction is shared.
- Preserve correction/void workflows if they are part of the verified product requirements.
25.9 Notifications
- Bell/inbox for connection requests, transaction proposals, decisions, and relevant system activity.
- Newest first; unread state and useful empty/loading/error states.
- Only show notifications from the last seven days.
- Notifications older than seven days must be automatically deleted by a trusted scheduled process or configured TTL—not merely hidden in the browser.
- Notification cleanup must never delete the underlying transaction or proposal.
- Do not duplicate notifications on retries; use idempotency keys.
- Do not include sensitive ledger details in notification payloads.
25.10 Settings (settings.html)
- Profile and account controls.
- Dark-only appearance (no theme toggle).
- Payment methods.
- Budget mode and budget inputs.
- Categories/tags.
- Cash-out fee configuration where applicable.
- Backup/restore and import report.
- Privacy/sharing controls.
- Account/data management and destructive actions.
Use compact grouped rows. Open detailed controls only after the user chooses a section. Confirm destructive actions. Avoid showing every setting and explanation at once.
25.11 Public shared-ledger view (share_view.html)
- Standalone, read-only view with minimal branding.
- Display only the specifically authorized person's ledger projection.
- Do not require access to the owner's private collection as a whole.
- Validate share tokens securely, do not enumerate tokens, and do not reveal owner UIDs or unrelated data.
- Escape all user-generated text.
- Provide a clear invalid/expired/revoked link state.
- Public sharing must be documented as a security-sensitive feature and must not be treated as safe merely because the URL is hard to guess.
26. Financial Correctness and Regression Tests
The existing blueprint's calculations and financial meanings are compatibility requirements. Before altering any formula, first identify its current behavior and add tests. Centralize formulas in a domain module.
26.1 Required wallet regression case
Use this known case as a mandatory regression test:
- Opening Cash: ৳1,040
- Opening bKash: ৳8,000
- Opening Nagad: ৳0
- Initial Wallet Total: ৳9,040
- Initial Net Total: ৳9,040
- User lends ৳500 to Emon from bKash.
Expected after the Give transaction:
- Cash: ৳1,040
- bKash: ৳7,400
- Nagad: ৳0
- Wallet Total: ৳9,040
- Net Total: ৳8,540
The difference is intentional: money moved out of the wallet into a receivable. The user must not see “You owe Emon ৳500” for this scenario.

This is a regression case from the existing product; preserve its meaning even as the payment-method data model changes.
26.2 Core calculation compatibility
The existing known behavior includes:
- Transfer cash-out fee: default rate 2% where applicable; fee is based on the stored rate and the historical transaction fields.
- A fee of 0% must be representable; do not use a fallback pattern that turns valid zero into 2%.
- Wallet balances derive from opening balances plus earnings minus expenses/recurring expenses and outgoing transfers plus incoming transfer effects, with personal ledger effects accounted for according to the established rules.
- Net Total differs from Wallet Total by the principal outstanding in personal Give/Take ledger calculations.
- Person ledger balance is calculated from the net difference of Give and Take under the established direction rules.
- Shared public view reverses perspective correctly for the counterparty.
- Budget logic uses the existing budget mode (direct, calculated, none) and current formula semantics. Verify actual source behavior before changing it.
- Recurring expense logic and historical fee semantics must not be reinterpreted during import.
Document formulas in DATA_MODEL.md and test edge cases including zero amounts if allowed, empty data, exact-zero balance, negative results where valid, rounding, dates around midnight in Asia/Dhaka, and duplicate method names.
26.3 Data and migration tests
Test:
- valid eco-backup v1 import;
- malformed JSON;
- unsupported format version;
- missing/unknown fields;
- tagged Firestore values;
- null versus missing fields;
- duplicate IDs;
- repeated import idempotency;
- redacted share hashes;
- legacy Cash/bKash/Nagad mapping;
- bKash present without Nagad;
- Nagad present without bKash;
- transaction referencing a method not found in method definitions;
- import verification count mismatch;
- interrupted import and safe resume/rollback.
27. Performance, Security, and Quality Gates
Before considering a page complete:
- Avoid unbounded collection reads; use pagination, date filters, or bounded summaries.
- Prevent stored XSS by safely rendering names, notes, tags, and proposal text.
- Never trust client-supplied owner IDs, amounts, proposal status, or permissions.
- Use Firestore Rules as the real authorization boundary; UI checks are not security.
- Avoid publicly readable private transaction/share documents.
- Do not use weak random token generation for public share tokens.
- Make duplicate submissions idempotent.
- Handle offline, permission-denied, missing-index, network, and malformed-data errors without losing user input.
- Show the actual failure reason in user-friendly language without exposing secrets.
- Test in a non-production Firebase project before owner deployment.
- Ensure no Firebase configuration or privileged secrets are fabricated.
- Keep logs free of sensitive personal financial information.
- Ensure keyboard accessibility, visible focus, semantic labels, reduced motion, and sufficient contrast.
- Confirm the PWA does not expose one user's cached financial data to another user on a shared device.
